<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Sitamau State</h1><p class="st-meta">1701 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Sitamau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Rathore Rajput state of Malwa, branch of the Ratlam house, whose prince Raghubir Sinh became a noted historian.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sitamau was founded in 1701 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keshav-das-of-sitamau">Keshav Das</a>, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a> family of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ratlam-state">Ratlam</a>, who received the territory as his own holding. Its rulers belonged to the same line that founded Ratlam and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sailana-state">Sailana</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Rajas ruled a small plateau territory of wheat and opium. In the 18th century the state paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scindias">Sindhia</a>, and after 1818 it came under British paramountcy as a salute state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa-agency">Malwa Agency</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raghubir-sinh">Maharaj Kumar Raghubir Sinh</a> founded a research library on Indian history at Sitamau.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Sitamau joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/madhya-bharat">Madhya Bharat</a> in 1948 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956. The Raghubir Library remains a centre for historical research in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandsaur">Mandsaur</a> district.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sitamau-state.jpg" alt="Sitamau State" width="218" height="150"><figcaption>Image: Xufanc · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sitamau_state_flag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1701 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sitamau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Keshav Das</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malvi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sitamau</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1824&amp;focus=sitamau-state" aria-label="Sitamau State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6686 1293 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sitamau State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7006 1549L7364 1601M7006 1549L7364 1602M7006 1549L7064 1471"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7064 1471h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7006 1549h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7006 1549h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sitamau. <a href="/atlas?year=1824&amp;focus=sitamau-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
